 ],
 "section_aids": {
  "1": "The introductory assertions lead to three different sorts of formal statement: definitions of the fields and estimates required, conditional results using those fields, and existence results supplying them. Theorem 1.1 includes uniqueness of the maximal velocity and lifespan. The later convergence result assumes a full inductive sequence carrying the step estimates of Theorem 7.1 for its velocities, residuals and oscillations, and does not construct those inputs.",
  "3": "The formal development follows the localized Craik–Criminale wave from its label profile to a physical divergence-free field. Transport identities, pathwise amplitude equations and curl completion are separate statements; a definition of one component does not assert the whole construction. The particular radial envelope and narrow phase family are fixed choices satisfying the paper’s requirements, so their formulas need not apply to every admissible profile mentioned in the exposition.",
  "4": "Section 4 solves a forced linear endpoint problem over a smooth incompressible parent that need not solve Euler. The unknown displacement has zero values at both endpoints; its initial velocity is selected by the problem. The main distinctions are between label and Eulerian forces, the displacement and velocity, and a pressure gradient versus an arbitrary scalar potential. The quadratic-form hypothesis is imposed, not derived from incompressibility.",
  "5": "The finite construction combines localized transverse corrections with global endpoint mean corrections. Its assumptions must accompany any later estimate: the parent bounds, the transported-frequency normalization, stability of every transverse homogeneous solution at the origin, positive coupling and a fixed small label radius. The formal parent pressure and peak-amplitude conditions are broader than the paper’s choices. A finite-depth theorem does not by itself choose the parameters of the later infinite iteration.",
  "7": "The numbered theorem constructs one sequence for every sufficiently large initial frequency after fixing the Fourier symbol, an envelope exponent above a prescribed floor, and fixed-order majorants; the threshold function is quantified after those choices. It identifies the localized expansion, Fourier truncation and oscillation at every step. The central amplitude is identified with the localized seed, but equality of the central and transported covectors and the first wave's directional and attained-normalization data are not among the exported conditions. Spatial Hölder estimates are given at each time; continuity in the corresponding time-dependent norm is not a separate clause.",
  "8": "The formal development separates selection of the common approximate sequence from construction of its continuous velocity limit on $[0,T_\\infty]$. The opening setting declaration contains no limiting field. The separate limit theorem proves uniform convergence and identifies the limit with the Euler solution before $T_\\infty$, taking Proposition 8.1 as input for that identification.",
  "Finite-Time Singularity Formation for the Three-Dimensional Euler Equations": "The main formal statement asserts existence of smooth odd divergence-free data on $\\mathbb T^3$ with a finite maximal lifespan, bounded velocity, central vorticity tending to infinity and the two divergent time integrals in Theorem 1.1. The torus is represented by integer-periodic fields on $\\mathbb R^3$. The local spatial limits away from the origin and the additional smooth space-time extension in Remark 1.2 are distinct conclusions. The latter does not impose Euler after the singular time.",
  "1.1": "The exact Craik–Criminale wave, its moving orthonormal frame and the finite-time amplification estimates are formalized separately. Nonvanishing and transversality must accompany the normalized frame. The reference angle $\\varphi=\\arctan(\\varepsilon\\tau^2/2)$ is distinguished from the angle of the actual perturbed wave. The return theorem compares the crossing strength with the attained maximum of the actual amplitude; its lower time endpoint comes from the reference solution. The strong-shear limit is a limit of the specified model, with a threshold uniform over its configurations.",
  "1.2": "The endpoint estimate is a coercivity statement for a forced linearized equation. For $0\\le T\\le1$, a path ending at zero and a quadratic-form upper bound $H\\le I$ give the factor $1/2$ after the terminal Poincaré inequality. The formal development first treats a single Hilbert-space path, then the label-dependent endpoint problem. The quantitative inverse uses $C^{0,1/2}$ fields and an explicit polynomial of degree $1164$. The solution norm includes the source norm as a factor. These statements do not assert least-action optimality for the nonlinear Euler flow.",
  "1.3": "The profiles depend separately on particle labels and phase, and the physical field is obtained only after composition with the inverse flow and the transported phase. The hierarchy cancels the parent residual together with the oscillatory interactions. Algebraic cancellation assumes solvers for the mean and transverse equations; analytic existence and bounds are supplied separately. At second order, the physical mean force is $-\\langle H_2\\rangle_s-R_U$, whose label version is its composition with the flow. The cutoff, finite depth and frequency lower bound belong to the remainder estimate.",
  "1.4": "The formal development of this unforced construction fixes the pressure coefficient by the Craik–Criminale equations and obtains the next orientation from their evolution. It does not supply the external rotation control described for the earlier forced constructions. The recorded central-gradient identity includes an error bounded by $\\lambda_j^{-1/4}$ and does not assume a neighborhood on which the corrected velocity is exactly linear. These statements support the mathematical distinction drawn here; they do not formalize or verify the separate blow-up constructions cited in the historical comparison.",
  "Conventions": "Vectors use the Euclidean norm and velocity gradients act by $D_xu\\,v$, with entries $\\partial_j u_i$. Matrix norms are specified in the individual statements: the two-dimensional amplitude system can use the maximum norm, while physical gradients use the Euclidean operator norm. Closed-slab derivatives are one-sided at the endpoints. Functions are defined everywhere, but their equations and regularity are restricted to the stated domains. Division by zero is assigned zero; real square roots of negative arguments and derivative or integral expressions outside their required hypotheses also have specified default values. Those conventions never supply missing analytic hypotheses.",
  "1.5": "The parameter conditions identify $\\lambda_0=N$, $q_1=1$, $K(s)=320b(s+1)$ and $L_j=4\\log\\lambda_{j+1}$. Frequencies are real; derivative orders and separation exponents are nonnegative integers. The maximum rule chooses the greatest admissible order at each step. Lemma 1.3 needs positivity and the logarithmic lower bound for the threshold, together with the recurrence; it does not need the additional monotonicity and analytic-cost domination imposed on the final threshold in the paper. Its arithmetic conclusion therefore holds under fewer assumptions.",
  "2.1": "This is the exact affine-wave calculation on $\\mathbb R^3$: $u=Dx+\\mathbf aF(k\\cdot x)$, the pressure determined by $D'+D^2$ and the wave coefficient, and persistence of nonzero transverse data. It is not a periodic Euler velocity on the torus. The formal statement needs only the differentiability used by the calculation, rather than the paper’s smooth odd periodic normalized profile. The Euler identity permits $k=0$ with the assigned zero quotient; persistence explicitly starts from nonzero vectors.",
  "2.1.1": "The moving-frame identities follow from the two wave ODEs, nonzero perpendicular vectors and $\\operatorname{tr}B=0$. They require neither symmetry of $B'+B^2$ nor a derivative of $B$. The third axis is $E_1\\times E_2$, fixing the sign of $B_{32}$. The formal conclusion includes orthonormality and the derivative of $\\log(|k||\\mathbf a|)$; the separate derivatives of the two logarithmic norms are intermediate calculations, which have separate general derivative statements.",
  "2.2": "The formal amplification argument treats a prescribed preceding wave and a new wave evolving over $M=B+\\mathbf a_p\\otimes k_p+E_c$. All physical ODEs are imposed on a compact set $I$ containing the active interval. The reference limit, finite-error comparison, reference gain, coupling estimates and crossing are distinct assertions. A compact observation set larger than the physical interval is permitted, but the four error suprema must then be measured over that larger set.",
  "2.2.1": "The formal statements fix $\\sigma,\\nu,\\eta$ at activation, use $\\tau=r(t-t_0)$, and express the new wave in the preceding wave’s moving frame. Lemma 2.3 gives exact finite-shear equations and a uniform limit for fixed $\\varepsilon,T$; it does not give a polynomial shear threshold. Lemma 2.4 supplies polynomial bounds under the two explicit smallness inequalities and chooses one continuous error operator before the amplitude. Intermediate amplitude matrices act on $(-\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2)$, so their signs must be converted before comparison with the paper.",
  "2.2.2": "The Riccati problem is posed on $[0,\\infty)$ with $Y(0)=1$ and $0<\\varepsilon\\le1$. The formal result retains existence, uniqueness on that half-line, the bounded deviation representation, the unique turning time, the amplitude maximum and the gain. Values of the supplied real functions at negative times are unrestricted. The triangular normalized system gives the forward propagator estimate. General derivative and coefficient lemmas often need fewer hypotheses than the full reference theorem, which their notes state explicitly.",
  "2.2.3": "Dividing by the positive reference amplitude gives $h=\\alpha_2/a_{\\rm ref}$ and $l=-(\\alpha_1+Y\\alpha_2)/(a_{\\rm ref}\\cos\\varphi)$. The formal comparison yields positivity, ratio and norm errors, logarithmic closeness, and an invertible map between the transverse planes. Intermediate error bounds use the maximum norm on pairs; the physical propagator uses the Euclidean norm. The numbered result specifies the factor $252\\exp(1)S$. Recovering physical components requires the explicitly stated wave-coordinate bounds and transversality.",
  "2.2.4": "The couplings are $\\nu_+=\\widehat k\\cdot M\\widehat a$ and $\\eta_+=(\\widehat k\\times\\widehat a)\\cdot M\\widehat a$. Their rational formulas use the orientation of the frame and the first-coordinate sign convention. The links now distinguish those declared coupling formulas from decomposition and cross-product calculations inside their proofs. Lemma 2.7 receives no reference solution as input. It proves the positive lower bound and late-time tolerances in (2.19); it does not retain the proof’s sharper $O(\\varepsilon)$ assertion for the second relative error.",
  "2.2.5": "The numbered proposition supplies a crossing in $[\\tau_m,2/\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\varepsilon_+}]$, bounds every crossing there relative to one attained physical amplitude maximum, and gives the early-time gain factor. Its strength coefficients are $\\exp(-1)/4$ and $18\\exp(3)$. The older crossing statement receives a reference solution, places some crossing strictly inside the interval, and uses the weaker symmetric lower coefficient $1/(18\\exp(3))$. Neither statement asserts crossing uniqueness or identifies the physical maximum with the reference turning time.",
  "3.1": "The inverse particle map transports the phase and envelope, while the amplitude solves the transverse equation at each label. The formal covector equation and pointwise chain rules need less regularity than the paper’s smooth parent. Pathwise existence gives an amplitude for one label; smooth label dependence and periodic extension are additional conclusions. A real carrier need not lie in the integer lattice: localization away from the label-cell boundary permits smooth periodization.",
  "3.2": "The completion is the slow curl of a normalized phase potential. The full curl identity, its divergence cancellation and the integrated longitudinal formula are distinct assertions with different hypotheses. The chosen phase family retains the signed negative powers in $h^{1-j}$. Time activation is handled on the closed slab, including the first wave with constant cutoff one. Four linked construction declarations additionally assume seed regularity: $0\\le t_0<T$, $0<h\\le1$, envelope radius $0<\\ell_*<1/4$, and $M$, $\\kappa$ and $\\mathbf a$ jointly smooth on $[t_0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^3$ with $\\kappa\\ne0$.",
  "4.1": "The coefficient identities use $F=D_aX$, $M=DU\\circ X$, $K=F^TF$ and $\\Omega=F^T(M^T-M)F$. For a parent residual $R_U$, the relevant quantity is $H^\\#=(D^2p_U-DR_U)\\circ X$, not the pressure Hessian alone. Some helpers concern arbitrary operators or a single trajectory and therefore do not identify a supplied matrix with an actual flow derivative. The notes state those identifications where they are needed.",
  "4.2": "The formal coercivity theorem covers the full Sobolev test class by representing $z\\in H^1_0$ as the primitive of a zero-mean $L^2$ velocity. The constant is $[2D_-^2(1+\\|M\\|_\\infty)^2]^{-1}$ under $0<T\\le1$ and $H^\\#\\le I$. The elementary estimate uses $T^2/2$; no sharp Poincaré constant or generalized-flow minimization theorem is claimed. Algebraic and continuously differentiable helpers are identified separately from the full Sobolev conclusion.",
  "4.3": "The inverse theorem gives $\\|v\\|\\le A\\|L\\|$ for every continuous half-Hölder source and bounds the Leray-composed fixed-time inverse by the same $A$. The formal polynomial uses coefficient and first derivative suprema, weaker controls than the paper’s full $C^{1,1/2}$ norms. Uniqueness concerns displacement, velocity, the constant solenoidal field and the pressure gradient. Smooth forcing gives a newly reconstructed smooth normalized label pressure, without differentiating an arbitrary original potential.",
  "5.1": "The formal parent condition records the velocity and residual Fourier bands, deformation and gradient bounds, residual size and the one-sided inequality $D^2p_U-DR_U\\le I$. It does not impose the paper’s normalized Poisson pressure; a separate theorem supplies that choice. The frequency is the transported covector length at activation, not the original carrier length. Central stability quantifies over every transverse homogeneous solution; the formal amplitude normalization is only $0<\\gamma\\le1$.",
  "5.2": "Parent derivatives are controlled by Bernstein estimates, the differentiated flow equation and composition rules. Several helpers assume the polynomial velocity derivative bounds directly and thus need fewer hypotheses than the numbered proposition. The formal operator norms differ from the paper’s coordinate derivative sums by order-dependent constants. Radius weighting yields a fixed parent exponent at all orders; the exact weighted constant is a finite sum of the supplied derivative constants.",
  "5.3": "The mean estimate applies to every smooth solution with zero displacement at both endpoints on $[0,T]$. The transformed source is $g=F^T\\bar f$ and the label velocity is $v=F^{-1}B$. The half-Hölder estimate preserves derivative order; conversion to scaled supremum norms loses one source derivative. A sufficiently small fixed radius absorbs the inverse cost. The formal statements explicitly require or prove bounded derivative sets, since the real-supremum expressions alone do not certify analytic finiteness.",
  "5.4": "The transverse equation is $V_t+MV+\\kappa\\mathsf p_s=f$, with $\\kappa\\cdot V=0$ and zero phase averages. The development separates the algebraic pressure slope from the existence, smoothness and uniqueness of an actual normalized pressure. The central stability assumption concerns every homogeneous transverse solution. The numbered estimate uses profiles smooth on $[0,T]$; a separate estimate accepts data only on $[t_0,T]$. Division by $\\gamma$ follows label and phase differentiation. The transverse plane is represented directly as a subspace of $\\mathbb R^3$, without choosing an orthonormal two-dimensional coordinate basis.",
  "5.5": "The construction is finite. It keeps each $V_p$ with its curl correction $C_{p+1}$ and its phase-independent mean $B_p$. Stored correction indices are shifted by one from the paper. The terminal correction remains even though $V_{P+1}=B_{P+1}=0$. No statement here asserts convergence of an infinite expansion at fixed frequency.",
  "5.5.1": "At each level the mean is obtained before the transverse velocity, because the new mean transports the leading phase. The level-two mean source includes $-R_U\\circ X$, and the differentiated leading cutoff is separated as $R_{\\widetilde\\chi}$. The retained equations cancel exactly. Oscillatory velocities, pressures, corrections and sources vanish before activation and outside the envelope; the means need not vanish there. On that inactive region the remainder reduces to interactions between means and is phase independent, rather than zero.",
  "5.5.2": "The curl correction gains $\\lambda^{-1}$ with one extra label or phase derivative. Phase integration by parts removes the fast derivative from the mean source. The numerical induction states the recurrence and the derivative reserve explicitly: order $n$ at level $p$ requires orders through $n+3(p-1)+1$. The final construction supplies all orders for the same profiles. One exponent of $\\mathsf D$ works for every level and depth; the constants indexed by order and level may grow. The remainder estimate has no remaining parent factor.",
  "5.6": "Evaluation at $a=X_{t,0}(x)$ and $s=k_*\\cdot a$ produces the physical fields. The numbered derivative estimate takes a profile already defined for all labels and phases, smooth on $[0,T]$, with the combined translation law; the paper also derives this continuation. A separate fixed-time continuation statement uses the boundary collar supplied by compact interior support. The parent residual cancels exactly. The pressure estimate is an upper quadratic-form bound, whereas the central-gradient error uses operator norm. The flow proof requires two derivatives of the pulled-back oscillation and one of its mean; it does not export the stronger intermediate second-order mean estimate printed in the paper.",
  "5.7": "Fourier truncation constructs $u=S_\\Lambda V$ and its zero-mean Poisson pressure. The cutoff error is $\\Lambda^{2-P}(1+\\|V\\|_{C_tC^{P,1/2}})^2$, with a signed integer exponent. The residual and pressure-Hessian error both retain a contribution from $R_V$; the latter comes from $-\\operatorname{div}R_V$ in the pressure difference equation. Bounds at independent times justify adding the two time suprema. The generic cutoff helper accepts a smooth even symbol directly and takes the approximation's upper pressure bound as an input; the numbered proposition obtains that input from Lemma 5.9.",
  "6.1": "The formal statements first specify central covector and amplitude paths over $B_\\rho=2\\pi(e_1+\\rho e_3)\\otimes e_2$, then prove their existence and the estimates of Lemma 6.1. The attained normalization is part of the data. Several supporting results allow every real $\\rho$, every positive frequency, or even $T=0$; the numbered lemma retains $0<\\rho\\le1$, $\\lambda\\ge1$ and $T>0$. The central-wave statements do not by themselves construct the localized perturbation or attach its constant time cutoff.",
  "6.2": "The formal development separates prescribed matrix and wave data from the theorem constructing the growing wave. It uses the physical interval $[t_0,t_0+\\Theta/r]$, with $r=\\sqrt{2\\sigma\\nu}$, and selects the least crossing only within the bracket following the reference turn. The propagation constant is explicit: $252eS$. The normalizing scale $r$ differs from the exact frozen growth rate by $\\sqrt{1-\\nu/\\sigma}$. The comparison error is defined exactly as in (2.6), with suprema over the interval on which the data are given.",
  "7.1": "The analytic statement derives the parent-gradient bounds $\\|\\nabla U_j\\|\\le\\lambda_j^9$ and $\\|\\partial_t\\nabla U_j\\|\\le\\lambda_j^{18}$ from smooth periodic velocity, its Fourier support, its size and the projected momentum equation. Time derivatives at slab endpoints are one-sided. The half-length and coefficient estimates are separate scalar statements about the frequency schedule; they do not estimate a velocity until the analytic hypotheses have been supplied. The maximum rule uses nonnegative integer orders, while the negative fractional exponents in the time scales are real exponents.",
  "7.2": "The construction proof combines the supplied Section 5 results, the first-wave lemma, crossing estimates and scalar budgets to obtain the common approximate sequence and a positive accumulation time strictly before every stage endpoint. Many links in this proof point only to a scalar absorption inequality or to the final construction theorem, not to every intermediate analytic estimate in the quoted display. The same construction carries one comparison constant valid at every later stage and also eventual comparison for any fixed constant. Keeping these two assertions distinct prevents the eventual clause from being mistaken for a missing all-stage hypothesis.",
  "8.1": "The formal statement constructs a smooth odd periodic Euler solution on $[0,T_\\infty)$ and proves initial-data convergence and convergence in every spatial $C^{m,1/2}$ norm uniformly on earlier closed intervals. Its comparison with maximal solutions is conditional and ranges only over finite real lifespans; it does not supply the general maximal-solution existence theorem used in the paper. The supplied proofs reveal a sum over full spatial derivatives; the defining file for its underlying Hölder quotient is absent.",
  "8.2": "The formal Proposition 8.2 proves bounded velocity and the full central-vorticity limit, and additionally retains a lower bound throughout each late activation interval. Corollary 8.3 combines that bound with crossing-duration estimates to prove divergence of the vorticity integral, then the gradient integral. This establishes the construction-specific conclusion by a different argument from the periodic Beale–Kato–Majda criterion cited in the paper; that general criterion is not proved by the linked development.",
  "8.3": "For each compact set off the torus origin, the formal statement gives limits of $u$ and $\\partial_tu$ with all spatial derivatives. On periodic lifts the compact set avoids every integer lattice point. Shrinking supports handle the local velocity increments, while a separated pressure kernel handles the nonlocal term. These spatial limits do not themselves assert a jointly smooth extension through the terminal time.",
  "A": "The formal development solves the endpoint system on $[0,1]\\times\\mathbb T^3$, with unique displacement, divergence-free constant field and periodic pressure gradient, a polynomial velocity bound, and smoothness for smooth data. The numbered theorem keeps the paper's symmetry assumptions but allows time continuity in $C^{0,1/2}$ with bounded first spatial derivatives in place of $C_tC^{1,1/2}$. Its supplied coefficient bounds give a different size parameter; the paper's parameter controls it after suitable choices, without a reverse Hölder-norm comparison.",
  "A.1 Spatial freezing": "The formal statement inverts $v\\mapsto\\mathcal R(Bv)$ on the projected half-Hölder space, separately for spatial vectors and square-integrable time paths, and preserves every available higher Hölder class. It requires only a Lipschitz coefficient for the base inverse. Supporting statements separate coercivity, Fourier inversion, localization and continuation. The localization uses a Lipschitz distance cutoff and a far-source estimate on distances at most $r/4$; the paper writes a gradient bound and uses $r/2$. The same powers follow after the stated changes of constants, but those intermediate assertions are not identical.",
  "A.2 Recovering the velocity at each time": "The time-path inverse first supplies a velocity defined almost everywhere in time. The formal recovery argument uses compatible, injective weak operators and continuous fixed-time inverses to obtain a continuous representative, preserving its zero time mean and both displacement endpoints. It then recovers a periodic pressure gradient; the scalar potential is not the object of uniqueness. For smooth data, time integration and the fixed spatial estimates give the smooth recovered fields. Some linked declarations state abstract recovery steps; their specialization to the endpoint equation is made explicit in the comparisons.",
  "B": "On $\\mathbb T^3=\\mathbb R^3/\\mathbb Z^3$, the cutoff is the multiplier $\\psi(\\xi/\\Lambda)$. The formal statement separates support, parity and derivative identities from the kernel estimates that give the operator, Bernstein and tail bounds. It admits even symbols and every $\\Lambda>0$, while the paper assumes radiality and $\\Lambda\\ge1$. Bernstein uses every real normed target and counts each multi-index once. The numbered measurable-input bound is complex Banach-valued; a separate theorem gives the real-velocity bound. The tail exponent is the integer difference $s-M$. Higher spatial Hölder norms are described as sums for derivative tensors, but their defining file is omitted.",
  "C": "Write $K(s)=320b(s+1)$, $\\lambda_0=N$ and $q_1=1$. The formal development follows the maximum rule for $q_{j+1}$ and the recurrence $\\lambda_j=\\lambda_{j-1}^{K(q_j)}$: it proves the one-step order bounds, divergence of the frequencies and orders, and $4\\log\\lambda_{j+2}\\le(\\log\\lambda_{j-1})^2$. The frequency-growth lemma needs only $b\\ge1$ and $N>1$ with the recurrence. The full order and logarithmic conclusions also require the initial threshold condition, maximum rule and positive cubic logarithmic lower bound. Threshold monotonicity and domination of analytic constants are unnecessary for these arithmetic conclusions. Several phrase links point to intermediate proof steps rather than the displayed theorem conclusion.",
  "D": "The finite norms sum one term per label-and-phase multi-index, weighted by $\\ell_*^{|\\beta|}h^j$; the weighted form measures derivatives after division by the positive time amplitude. Appendix D then proves differentiation, product and phase-operator estimates, coefficient closure, and lifting of a zero-order estimate. The formal finite Hölder norm uses the maximum of uniform norm and seminorm, and coefficient powers have integer exponents. Rounding a fixed real exponent in D.2(c) changes the admissible radius. The lifting statement is abstract in solution and forcing spaces, so constraints, prescribed data and equations modulo gradients require an explicit interpretation. Its constant is uniform in derivative maps and preserves one zero-order inverse factor.",
  "Auxiliary note": "The proved auxiliary existence result chooses one threshold and a construction whose mixed estimates permit a smooth periodic velocity extension through the singular time away from the origin. It imposes Euler only before that time. Broader assertions for every qualifying threshold or construction are retained separately without proofs. The selected theorem shares a terminal time between its stages and solution; its proof uses the actual stage limit, although convergence is not exported in that theorem's conclusion.",
  "Auxiliary note 1": "The selected-construction theorems choose the threshold before the initial frequency and give eventual estimates at every fixed pair of orders, including continuity in the relevant spatial norm. Time derivatives fix the physical spatial point and are one-sided at stage endpoints. The generalized auxiliary statements range over more constructions and are marked as not proved in the authors' formalization; their hypotheses must not be silently identified with the proved selected construction.",
  "Auxiliary note 2": "The time induction advances velocity by one order while using the parent residual only through the preceding order. Factorial time weights are taken before the amplitude weighting, and the mean inverse spends one additional label derivative while gaining one source time derivative. Several links export only raw derivative bounds or summed-profile bounds, rather than every estimate named in the paper. The final frequency absorption and cutoff steps require their stated fixed-order hypotheses; exact imported spatial-seminorm formulas remain unavailable here.",
  "Auxiliary note 3": "Eventual mixed correction bounds, shrinking supports and uniform velocity convergence give joint smoothness up to the terminal slice off the lattice and local convergence of every joint derivative. The extension theorem then gives a periodic velocity extension to all real times off the origin. This is an existence result for an extension of each field; it does not state the continuous linear extension operator of Seeley's original theorem or impose Euler after the singular time."
 },
 "reading_aids": {
  "showcase": true,
  "route": true
 }
}
